# TCP vs UDP: Key Differences and When to Use Each

## Core Differences

| Aspect | TCP | UDP |
|--------|-----|-----|
| **Connection** | Connection-oriented (3-way handshake) | Connectionless |
| **Reliability** | Guaranteed delivery, ordering, error checking | Best-effort; no guarantees |
| **Header size** | 20–60 bytes | 8 bytes |
| **Speed** | Slower (overhead from ACKs, retransmits) | Faster, lower latency |
| **Flow/Congestion control** | Yes (windowing, slow start, etc.) | No |
| **Data boundaries** | Byte stream (no message boundaries) | Datagram-based (preserves boundaries) |
| **Ordering** | Guaranteed in-order delivery | No ordering guarantee |
| **Broadcast/Multicast** | Not supported | Supported |
| **Use case** | Web, email, file transfer, SSH | Streaming, gaming, DNS, VoIP |

## How They Work

**TCP** establishes a connection via a three-way handshake (SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK), numbers every segment, acknowledges receipt, retransmits lost packets, and reassembles data in order. It also adapts its sending rate to network conditions (congestion control).

